{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\nSummary:\n1. Primary Request and Intent:\n   The user invoked a cobbler-scaffold stitch task (#5537) for the go-unix-utils project as part of generation-run44. This is an automated code generation pipeline where a \"measure\" phase proposes requirements, and a \"stitch\" phase implements them. The task requires implementing specific Go utility commands based on Software Requirements Documents (SRDs). The task instructions say to: (1) Review provided context, (2) Plan approach, (3) Implement the work described in Requirements and Files to Create/Modify, (4) Verify acceptance criteria. However, the explicit \"Requirements\" and \"Files to Create/Modify\" sections were not clearly delineated in the prompt — they need to be determined from the requirements.yaml file by finding which requirements were marked `in_progress` for task #5537.\n\n2. Key Technical Concepts:\n   - Go reimplementations of GNU coreutils/moreutils utilities\n   - Differential testing against Homebrew GNU reference binaries (g-prefixed: gcat, gls, etc.)\n   - pkg/testutils.RunDiffTests pattern for comparing Go binary vs reference binary output\n   - SIGPIPE handler via sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() required in all cmd/ utilities\n   - exec.LookPath + t.Skip pattern for graceful handling of missing reference binaries\n   - LC_ALL=C environment for all differential tests\n   - BuildBinary(t, \".\") for compiling cmd/ packages within the module\n   - Strict execution constitution: no git commands, 40-line function limit, 500-line file limit, DRY enforcement, copyright headers required\n   - Write-first implementation strategy: write all files before building/testing\n   - Many SRDs covering utilities from trivial (true, false) to complex (ls, sort, stat)\n   - Shared packages: pkg/testutils, pkg/sys, pkg/format, pkg/hashutil, pkg/sizeparse, pkg/encutil\n\n3. Files and Code Sections:\n   - `.cobbler/requirements.yaml` (first 100 lines read):\n     - This file tracks requirement states per SRD. Critical for determining what work is assigned to this task.\n     - First 100 lines showed srd001-testutils requirements (R1.1-R5.2) with statuses: complete (R1.1-R2.4), uncertain (R2.5-R5.2)\n     - Need to read further to find requirements with status \"in_progress\" for task #5537\n   - All pkg/ source files were provided inline in the prompt (testutils, sys, format, hashutil, sizeparse, encutil) - these define the shared library contracts\n   - requirement_states in the prompt show many SRDs with \"ready\" status: srd004-ts, srd083-truncate, srd089-srd114 (chmod through vidir)\n\n4. Errors and fixes:\n   - No errors encountered yet. Work was interrupted at the very first step (reading requirements.yaml to determine task scope).\n\n5. Problem Solving:\n   - The core problem not yet solved: determining WHICH specific requirements are assigned to task #5537. The git log shows \"Mark requirements in_progress for #5537\" was committed, meaning some requirements in requirements.yaml were transitioned from \"ready\" to \"in_progress\". Only the first 100 lines were read, showing srd001-testutils requirements (all complete or uncertain, none in_progress). The in_progress requirements are likely further in the file.\n\n6. All user messages:\n   - Message 1: Massive stitch task prompt containing full project context, execution constitution, go style constitution, all source code, requirement states, package contracts, shared protocols, and task instructions for implementing requirements assigned to task #5537.\n   - Message 2: \"CRITICAL: Respond with TEXT ONLY. Do NOT call any tools.\" - Request to summarize the conversation.\n\n7. Pending Tasks:\n   - Read the rest of `.cobbler/requirements.yaml` to find which requirements have status \"in_progress\" (the