{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_011HevbEkgdPVGxoxG4JYT15","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a thorough understanding of GNU truncate behavior. Let me implement the full cmd/truncate utility covering all SRD requirements (R1.1-R3.3). The SRD specifies:\n\nR1.1: -s SIZE / --size=SIZE with unit suffixes\nR1.2: SIZE prefix modifiers: +, -, <, >, /, %\nR1.3: Multiple FILE arguments\nR1.4: -c / --no-create to suppress file creation\nR2.1: -r RFILE / --reference=RFILE for reference file sizing\nR2.2: Exit 1 if reference file cannot be accessed\n\n\nR3.1: Exit 0 on success\nR3.2: Exit 1 on any error\nR3.3: SIGPIPE handler via sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler\n\nI've tested the behavior extensively and need to handle specific error messages: missing size or reference argument, missing file operand, invalid size formats, and various file access errors. The -c flag should silently skip nonexistent files while still exiting successfully, and when processing multiple files, I need to report the first error but continue processing all files before returning the final exit code.\n\nI'm going to stick with what the SRD specifies rather than trying to match every quirk of the GNU implementation, since the differential testing will be separate. The error message format should use the binary name as a prefix, and I need to carefully parse the SIZE argument according to the specification.\n\nFor SIZE parsing, I'll need to handle the prefix modifiers manually since sizeparse only supports + and - signs. I'll check for the truncate-specific prefixes (<, >, /, %) first, then use sizeparse with AllowSign=true to handle the numeric portion.\n\nActually, looking at GNU truncate's format more carefully—SIZE is just [prefix]number[suffix] where prefix is exactly one of the six characters, not a combination. So I should extract whichever prefix is present, then pass the remainder to sizeparse without sign handling, since the prefix itself determines the operation.\n\nWriting the parsing logic... I'm realizing file sizes can't actually be negative, so I need to reconsider how the minus operation should work—it should probably clamp to zero rather than allowing negative values.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":880,"cache_read_input_tokens":50303,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":880},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a61810ff-70f4-4e3b-8473-0c1f0b2a6146","uuid":"d47ea27d-e8f1-477c-ba74-4d5ca67856ad"}
